  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 1238 AM CDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nDespite the Panhandle thermostat getting turned up through most of \nthe week, global modals have been hinting that Wed - Thu could have \npotential for more rain chances and \"cooler\" temperatures. The \nculprit for such potential is a trailing trough on the southeast \nflank of a large upper level low over the Hudson Bay of Canada. \nThis trough will dive southeast across the Plains late Wednesday \ninto Thursday, placing northwest flow atop the region. A weak \nfrontal passage will make its way over the area as well, resulting\nin better moisture content and temperatures dropping a few \ndegrees by Thursday (highs mostly in the 90s). Model agreement has\nimproved regarding this potential, which places 20-40% storm \nchances across the north and western combined Panhandles primarily\nWed evening and overnight. CAPE and shear profiles may be \nsufficient for a low-end severe threat as well, but we will have \nto wait and see how the thermodynamic environment pans out. \n\nUnfortunately, high pressure is progged to translate from the Four \nCorners region back to the Southern Great Plains late week. \nWidespread triple digit heat promptly returns to end the week \nthrough the weekend, and dry air will filter back to the region in\ntandem. Rain chances will be slim to none for several days, as it\nappears high pressure is here to stay. \n\nHarrel",
